## Safe area (notch / cutout) — required in BOTH orientations

- Manifest/window: `LAYOUT_IN_DISPLAY_CUTOUT_MODE_SHORT_EDGES`.
- Apply padding via `setOnApplyWindowInsetsListener`:
  - **portrait** → top inset (status bar + top notch),
  - **landscape** → left + right insets (side cameras).
- Re-apply on `onConfigurationChanged` and after lock/unlock.
- The CSS safe-area kill in the page is SEPARATE and must NOT use `*` selectors
  (see pitfalls #10) — only `:root` vars + named root containers.

---

## Keyboard must not cover inputs

**Read `kotlin_keyboard.mdc` and implement what it says.** Do not improvise
here — `adjustResize` plus a JS `scrollIntoView` looks correct in a five-minute
test and jitters visibly in real use, because the engine and the injection then
scroll the same page and their corrections add up.

The shape of the solution: keep `adjustResize` in the manifest so the animation
callback fires, strip the IME insets before they reach the WebView, and slide
the view with `translationY` by an amount computed from the field position the
page reports over a JS bridge, clamped to the height the keyboard settles at.

---

## Loading session and the cover

Two different mechanisms, never mixed up:

- **The loading screen (splash + bar)** belongs to the launch. One per launch,
  shown by the router, filled to 100% before anything replaces it. It must
  never reappear: not after the notification-permission screen, not for a push
  URL on a live shell, not between redirect hops.
- **The cover** belongs to the WebView. It is a scrim over the current page
  (dimmed for an ordinary navigation, opaque after a failed load) with a small
  spinner, raised in `onPageStarted` and dropped a beat after `onPageFinished`.
  That beat matters: a redirect hop finishes and starts the next load within a
  frame or two, and without the linger the cover blinks off and on between
  them.

Back the cover with a timeout so a page that never reports back cannot hold the
screen for good, and drop it on the paths that never produce a finished page —
a load that turns into a download, an unsupported scheme handed to the system.

**Never snapshot the WebView to build a freeze-frame.** `view.draw(canvas)` on
a hardware-accelerated WebView yields a black bitmap, which is exactly the
"black screen during redirects" the client reported. A scrim over the live view
costs nothing and cannot be black.

---

## Redirect loop (ERR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS)

Chromium gives up after 20 hops; affiliate chains are routinely longer. Track
the last main-frame URL in `shouldOverrideUrlLoading` and, on a main-frame
`onReceivedError` with `ERROR_REDIRECT_LOOP` / `ERROR_TOO_MANY_REDIRECTS` (or a
description containing `too_many`), reload **the deepest hop seen** — not the
original URL — so the chain resumes instead of restarting. Up to 6 attempts,
with only a ~60 ms pause: this is dead time in the middle of a navigation.

Two flags make the retry actually work, and both were bugs before they were
flags:

- `loadFailed`, set in `onReceivedError` and cleared in `onPageStarted`. A
  failed load still reaches `onPageFinished`, with the error page committed. If
  that path resets the retry counter and records the URL as settled, the budget
  never runs down and the error page is handed to the user.
- `retryPending`, set while a reload is queued. The cover must not drop in that
  gap, or the native error page flashes.

