## Renderer crashes

`onRenderProcessGone` fires when the renderer dies; returning `false` kills the
app. Return `true`, destroy the dead view, build a fresh WebView with the same
settings, re-bind the keyboard pan to it, and reload the last settled URL. Cap
the recoveries so a page that kills the renderer on sight cannot loop.

---

## shouldOverrideUrlLoading routing

Whitelist the web schemes, keep them in the WebView, and hand **everything
else** to the system. Partner pages link to banks, wallets, messengers and
stores — `paytmmp:`, `upi:`, `tg:`, `whatsapp:` and dozens more — and a WebView
handed one of those shows `ERR_UNKNOWN_URL_SCHEME`. Enumerating the schemes you
happen to know is a losing game; invert the test.

```kotlin
val scheme = url.substringBefore(':').lowercase()
when {
    scheme in setOf("http", "https", "about", "data", "blob", "file", "javascript") -> {
        if (isMainFrame) lastMainFrame = url
        false                                  // stays in the WebView
    }
    scheme == "intent" -> { openIntentUri(url); true }
    else -> { openExternally(url); true }      // any app link
}
```

`intent://` URIs need their own handling: parse with
`Intent.parseUri(url, URI_INTENT_SCHEME)`, clear `component`/`selector`, add
`CATEGORY_BROWSABLE` and `FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK`, try it, then retry with
`package = null` (the named app may be missing while another handles the
scheme), and finally load `browser_fallback_url` if the URI carries one.

When the user comes back, they must land on the page they left — no error page.
An `ERROR_UNSUPPORTED_SCHEME` reaching `onReceivedError` means the URL was
already handed to the system, so just drop the cover and leave the page alone.

